<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">German Labour Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Deutsche Arbeitsfront</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The German Labour Front was the Nazi organisation that replaced the trade unions after they were dissolved in May 1933. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ley">Robert Ley</a>, it enrolled employers and workers together, reaching more than 20 million members, and controlled wages, workplace discipline and leisure through branches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strength-through-joy">Strength Through Joy</a>. It also ran businesses, including the Volkswagen works.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-labour-front.jpg" alt="German Labour Front" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dagData from Davis/Mc Gregor: &quot;Flags of the Third Reich&quot; Part 3, London 1994, ISBN 1 85532 459 8 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deutsche_Arbeitsfront.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>As early as March 1933, two months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became Chancellor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmabteilung">Sturmabteilung</a> began to attack trade union offices without legal consequences. Offices were occupied, furnishings destroyed, documents stolen or burned, and union members beaten, thrown out of windows and in some cases killed, while the police declared themselves without jurisdiction. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> leadership adopted a general policy only in May. On 2 May 1933 union headquarters throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> were occupied, their funds confiscated and the unions abolished, and their leaders were beaten and arrested.</p><p>Many were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. The German Labour Front, the Deutsche Arbeitsfront, was created in May 1933 to take over the seized assets, and Hitler appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ley">Robert Ley</a>, who had no previous experience of labour relations, to lead it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Three weeks later Hitler issued a decree banning collective bargaining and appointing labour trustees to regulate labour contracts, which in effect outlawed strikes. Ley promised to restore absolute leadership in a factory to the employer. Unlike the unions, the Front also included employers and members of the professions, and its founding law said its aim was not to protect workers but to create a social and productive community of all Germans.</p><p>The trustees followed the wishes of employers, and Hitler wanted wages kept low. In February 1935 a workbook system was introduced: the employer kept each worker's book and could refuse to release it, which prevented the worker from being legally employed elsewhere.</p><p>To offset these restrictions the Front offered leisure through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strength-through-joy">Strength Through Joy</a>, which provided concerts, adult education, subsidised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and opera tickets and cheap holidays, especially cruises. The number of people taking holiday cruises rose from 2.3 million in 1934 to 10.3 million in 1938. At Prora on Rügen it began a seaside complex 4.5 kilometres long, planned for 20,000 beds, which was never finished. It also subsidised a car factory, partly from payroll deductions, but none of the 340,000 workers paying for a Volkswagen received one, since the factory was turned to war production after the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Membership was in theory voluntary, but workers found it hard to get a job without it, and many unemployed people were drafted into the Front, given uniforms and tools and put to work. By 1939 it had over 35,000 full-time employees. It ran the Bank of German Labour and programmes of medical screening, occupational training and legal assistance, and the Beauty of Labour organisation, which aimed to improve workplaces with renovations and new canteens. It also organised the Reichsberufswettkampf, a national vocational competition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German Labour Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Independent organisations were replaced by Nazi bodies such as the <b>German Labour Front</b> under Robert Ley, the Hitler Youth and the Strength through Joy leisure organisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
